  "worked_solution": "We're given that $y=\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(2)=\\frac{3}{4}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{A}{{x}^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{A}{{2}^{4}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{A}{16} \\\\ A & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
